# Humanize_AI

A guide for writing slides, documents and messages that read as if a careful person wrote them. It condenses an AI-slop taxonomy, its worked examples, and the lessons from ten graded slop-review slides.

**How to use this file.** Give it to any AI before it writes or edits text. The AI drafts in plain words, then runs the line check in Section 8 on every title, label, bullet, table cell and note. It rewrites any line that fails, and deletes the line if nothing useful remains.

---

## 0. The one question

Ask of every line: **Could a person say this more plainly?**

Two quick tests answer it:

- **Say it out loud.** If nobody would say it that way in a meeting, rewrite it.
- **Ask "which one?"** Which product, which number, which customer, of what, to what. A line that cannot answer says nothing.

---

## 1. The standard

Every line should be clear, natural and easy for its intended reader to follow.

| Penalize | Reward |
|---|---|
| Confusing, awkward, vague or needlessly elaborate language | Direct, precise language with no extra complexity |
| Generic or repetitive filler, empty slogans, text that talks about itself | Natural phrasing and terms that suit the reader |
| Jargon, big words, formulaic phrasing, heavy punctuation where plain words work | Short writing that keeps the meaning and avoids repeats |

---

## 2. Hard bans

These patterns count as slop every time they appear, whatever the rest of the sentence says.

### 2.1 Punctuation

| Mark | Problem example | Plain fix |
|---|---|---|
| Em dash `—` | "Jobs fell 38% from February to April 2020, then climbed back gradually—ending 2024 still just below the pre-shock level." | Two sentences, or a comma |
| Spaced hyphen or en dash used as a pause | "This isn't just a calendar - it's a gateway" | One plain sentence |
| Semicolon `;` | "Travel demand and employment fell within weeks; persistent behavior shifts are shaping the path back." | Two sentences |
| Vertical bar `\|` | "Strategy \| Execution \| Impact" and "+$3.3 \| +10.8%" | Commas, "and", or separate table cells |
| Arrows `→` `->` `-->` | "Human --> cell activation --> measurable luciferase signal" | A sentence with a real verb |
| In-line dots `•` `·` | "Faster growth • Better efficiency • Stronger outcomes" | Commas, or a real bulleted list |
| Slash joining two labels | "CASH FLOW / HISTORICAL CASH GENERATION" | One label, or join with "and" |
| Colon chains | "Identified goods: wine. This is the closest possible conflict: same mark and same goods." | Full sentences, at most one colon per line |
| "e.g." together with "etc." | "e.g. cruise, casinos, hotels, etc." | Keep one of the two |

Allowed:

- Number ranges with "to" or a plain hyphen: "300 to 400 mg", "FY20-FY22".
- A slash inside an established product or legal name, such as "bio-LNG/biomethane".
- One colon that opens a list, or a label such as "Source:".

### 2.2 Contrast frames

Never build a line on a denial followed by a correction. Every one of these shapes counts:

| Shape | Example |
|---|---|
| "X is Y, not Z" | "Humidity is an exposure trajectory, not a setpoint" |
| "This isn't just X, it's Y" | "This isn't just a calendar - it's a gateway to a more intentional life." |
| The two-sentence version | "Data access is not a background detail. It's the heart of the user experience." |
| "Do Y, not X" | "Screen the energy credit, not the headline retail tariff" |
| "Y rather than X" | "Profile consumers by purchasing power, needs and behaviors rather than treating the segment as uniform." |

The fix: state the positive claim on its own and support it with a fact.

### 2.3 Passive voice

Name the actor and put it first. A sentence where the subject receives the action hides who does the work.

| Passive | Active |
|---|---|
| The proposal was approved by the team. | The team approved the proposal. |
| The launch was delayed. | The team delayed the launch. |
| Maintenance is priced up front and linked to asset condition. | The partner prices maintenance up front and ties it to asset condition. |
| Current owner and filing date should be confirmed from TSDR. | Counsel should confirm the current owner and filing date in TSDR. |
| Tribal stakeholders are expected to continue scrutinizing ePull Tabs. | Experts expect tribal stakeholders to keep scrutinizing ePull Tabs. |
| Can deliver low lifecycle emissions when methane slip is controlled. | Can deliver low lifecycle emissions when operators control methane slip. |

Words to hunt: is, are, was, were, be, been or being, followed by a past participle such as priced, used, maintained, assigned, expected, proposed, confirmed, linked, regarded, controlled, received, generated or designed. Always name the real actor from the source.

### 2.4 Command-style titles and headers

A title or section header describes what the slide shows. It never orders the reader to act.

| Command | Description |
|---|---|
| Define the integration perimeter before designing the workplan | Integration perimeter comes before the workplan |
| Screen the energy credit, not the headline retail tariff | The energy credit decides the screening result |
| Scale the network, maintain quality | Network growth and service quality |
| Build the ESG management foundation | ESG management system |
| Understand demand | Consumer demand |
| Adapt go-to-market | Sales and distribution |

Action items work differently. Inside a list of next steps, a bullet may start with a verb, and it must then name the exact task and object. "Validate thermal spacing, wire fill, service clearances, and final breaker sizing" fails because the reader cannot tell which spacing or which clearances.

### 2.5 Slogan-like phrasing

If a line would work on a billboard or in an ad, it is a slogan. Replace it with the fact it hides.

| Shape | Examples |
|---|---|
| Two-fragment taglines | "Distinct strengths. One integrated Ford." "Investor value, measurable impact" |
| Catchy segment taglines | "Freedom for every day", "Built to move business", "Always on, always learning" |
| Punchy fragment openers | "Broad pyrogen coverage." at the start of a bullet |
| Buzzword triads | "scale, speed and certainty", "stronger systems, a scalable sustainability culture and credible market readiness" |
| Promise language | "ideally positioned to seize market growth opportunities", "at the forefront of digital transformation", "presenting a superior company to the market" |
| Formula headers | "Why magnesium matters", "What drove the quarter", "UNLOCKING POTENTIAL" |
| Short true claims with no detail | "Automation increases efficiency" |
| Polished statements that say little | "Usage-based models turn customer activity into a direct revenue expansion path" |

