## 3. The five wording problems

### 3.1 Awkward or unnatural wording

- **Question:** Would a person naturally phrase it this way?
- **Test:** Read it aloud and ask, "Would I ever say this?"
- **Flag when:** a phrase is technically understandable but sounds assembled by a machine and breaks the flow.

Common causes:

- A verb that does not fit its noun: improve income, roll out a culture, claims return, reduce interfaces, respect frugality.
- An adjective that does not fit its noun: unusually high change, strong suitability.
- Invented compounds and squeezed labels: value-led, five-question pulse, POST-COVID CONSUMER, demand-risk allocation, digital discovery.
- Stacks of three or more nouns: safety performance level, training-coordinator alignment, healthy nervous system function.
- Fancy verbs where a plain one exists: anticipate, pair, frame, warrant.
- Grammar slips: "certificates attracts", "personal professional growth", "untraditional way of evaluating", a bullet with no subject.

Short labels deserve the same scrutiny as long sentences.

| Awkward | Plain version |
|---|---|
| "Shopping has become more deliberate and value-led." | "Shopping has become more deliberate and focused on value." |
| "A closer 2021 step-up from a small base" | "A larger increase in 2021 from a low baseline" |
| "A five-question pulse uses simple 0-10 scores and one verbatim follow-up" | "The five-question survey uses a 0-10 scale and one open-ended question" |
| "consumers are thinking harder before they buy" | "consumers have become more deliberate about purchases" |
| "POST-COVID CONSUMER" | "Consumers after COVID-19" |
| "Unusually high NWC change" | "Unusually large NWC change" |
| "c.150 Consulting FTEs" | "c.150 consultants" |
| "Circadian clocks anticipate the 24-hour day" | "Circadian clocks predict the 24-hour day" |
| "Four lanes pair stepper pick-and-place motion with servo lift-axis adjustment" | "Each of the four lanes combines stepper pick-and-place motion with a servo lift axis" |
| "A partner can improve commercial income" | "A partner can increase commercial income" |
| "Claims return to the city if scope, utilities or relief events are unclear" | "The city becomes responsible for claims if scope, utilities or relief events are unclear" |
| "Roll out a consistent sustainability culture across five African countries" | "Build a consistent sustainability culture across five African countries" |
| "Growing Asian cuisine to fuel demand" | "Growing interest in Asian cuisine raises demand" |
| "alcoholic beverages identified apart from wine" | "alcoholic beverages other than wine" |
| "Sustainability certificates attracts premium retailers" | "Sustainability certificates attract premium retailers" |
| "Confirm I/O list and safety performance level" | "Confirm the I/O list and operating safety" |

### 3.2 Excessive or flowery wording

- **Question:** Is a simple idea dressed up as something grand?
- **Test:** Strip the decoration. If the plain version says the same thing in fewer words, use the plain version.
- **Flag when:** jargon and abstraction stand in for a concrete statement. This is consultant language that complicates things for no reason.

Also flag sentences that pack in too much. If one bullet holds more than three documents, references or steps, split it.

| Inflated | Plain version |
|---|---|
| "The organization seeks to holistically cultivate a multifaceted ecosystem of synergistic capabilities that meaningfully facilitates the realization of enhanced outcomes." | "The organization seeks to build a cohesive set of capabilities that will improve its outcomes." |
| "stronger AI performance for next-generation flagship smartphones" | "stronger AI performance for flagship smartphones" |
| "A structurally lower commute base." | "A permanent drop in commuting" |
| "Reduced overhead through scalable spans and layers" | "Reduced overhead through streamlined management structures" |
| "Catalyzing Transformative Excellence" | "Performance Improvement" |
| "Plans account for a softer U.S. demand environment." | "Plans account for lower U.S. demand." |
| "Four mutually reinforcing demand engines" | "Four growth drivers" |
| "Replace an animal response with a mechanistic human-cell readout while maintaining patient-protective pyrogen detection." | "Replace the rabbit test with a human-cell test that still detects pyrogens." |
| "Expertise as an organizational development and change catalyst" | "Leads organizational change" |
| "Performs automated analysis in real time." | "Analyzes [name the input] in real time." |
| "A defined governance process is in place to monitor capability development." | "A governance process monitors capability development." |
| "Would provide a strong basis for opposition or a demand to stop use." | "Would give strong grounds to oppose use of the mark." |
| "ICONIC / IKONIQ-type near-equivalent federal record(s)" | "ICONIC- and IKONIQ-like federal records" |
| "If you have multiple buildings in the same market, and a long-term perspective there are times where you might consider choosing growth potential over credit security" | "Owners with several buildings in one market may choose growth potential over credit security" |
| "Strong suitability for product innovation efforts of large retail and corporate clients" | "Suits product development work for large retailers and corporate clients" |

### 3.3 Vague or empty wording

- **Question:** Do the words convey anything specific?
- **Test:** Ask which product, which metric, which outcome. If the slide cannot answer, the words are empty.
- **Flag when:** the line never names the specific thing it stands for, or it could sit unchanged on almost any other slide.

Patterns to catch:

- Adjectives with no measure: broad-based, consistent, strong, superior, balanced, attractive, meaningful, significant, very large.
- Nouns with no "of what": volume, certification, bottlenecks, availability, the category, the core, this practice.
- One-word abstract labels: Persistence, Resetting, Endogenous timing.
- Broad noun pairs: "behavior and physiology", "design, technology and operations", "a wide range of uses".
- Metaphors that hide the claim: anchor, momentum, engines, tailwind, headwind, fuel, lens, foundation, broadening, deepen.
- Lines that promise a figure and give none: "Percentage of graduates employed within six months*".

| Vague | Plain version |
|---|---|
| "FY24 revenue $2.68B (+26%), with broad-based usage across products." | "FY24 revenue $2.68B (+26%), with usage increasing across products." Name the products when the data allows. |
| "More consistent coverage and stronger transparency" | "Improved staffing coverage and scheduling visibility" |
| "Price and availability lead transportation adoption" | "Price and availability are the main drivers of transport adoption" |
| "Strategic Value Drivers" on a revenue slide | "Revenue Metrics" |
| "Expected decline in U.S. volume" | "Expected decline in U.S. sales volume" |
| "Supply, liquefaction capacity and certification remain the bottlenecks." | "Biomethane supply, liquefaction capacity and fuel certification limit adoption." |
| "Persistence" as a label | "Rhythms persist in constant darkness" |
| "Circadian time in constant darkness" as an axis label | "Hours in constant darkness" |
| "Magnesium helps the body stay balanced under everyday stress" | "Magnesium helps regulate cortisol during stress" |
| "Managers and operators have confirmed this practice." | "Managers and operators confirmed that tracking covers only GMP and onboarding." |
| "Automation increases efficiency" | "[What is automated] cuts [which cost or time] by [amount]" |
| "Percentage of graduates employed within six months*" | Give the percentage, or delete the line |

### 3.4 Unnecessary wording

- **Question:** Can this word or phrase go with no loss?
- **Test:** Delete it and reread. If the meaning holds, it was never doing any work.
- **Flag when:** a modifier repeats what a neighboring word already says, or a label decorates the slide without making a point.

Patterns to catch:

- Modifiers a neighbor already implies: practical, next-generation, drop-in, existing, expert, defined, key, conceptual, illustrative, "future" in a forward-looking line.
- Synonym pairs: risk / cost, "lock in standards or constrain change", "partnerships ... strategic alliances", "software & digital", "green or ESG", "near-term ... imminent", "localize ... to local needs", "complementary engines work together".
- Repeats: a kicker or section header that restates the title, a bullet that restates its own header, a closing line that restates the title, the same label on every row.
- Meta text about the document itself: "Internal planning discussion", "Internal planning draft", "Delivery model briefing", "Strategic briefing".
- Hedging disclaimers and notes about the source: "This executive summary does not replace a current TSDR pull...", "Source material does not specify...".
- Labels the brief never asked for: department kickers, "Regulatory intent", "ESG mandate", "Growth Strategy", "DAILY WELLBEING", "MODEL LENS FOR THIS HUB".
- Filler endings: "to reveal what matters most".

| Padded | Plain version |
|---|---|
| "Practical consumer needs consistently outweigh broader supporting factors" | "Consumer needs consistently outweigh broader supporting factors" |
| "one verbatim follow-up to reveal what matters most" | "one open-ended follow-up" |
| "UNLOCKING POTENTIAL" | Delete |
| "DROP-IN DIESEL BIOFUELS" | "DIESEL BIOFUELS" |
| "Closest substitute for marine gasoil / diesel in existing engines" | "Closest substitute for marine gasoil and diesel" |
| "2 practices and 4 expert brands" | "2 practices and 4 brands" |
| "PPP RISK / COST" | "PPP RISKS" |
| "A long-term contract may lock in standards or constrain change." | "A long-term contract may lock in standards." |
| "Electric, software & digital" | "Electric and software" |
| "PEAK SEASON 2025" above the title "2025 Peak Season Outlook" | Delete the kicker |
| "Federal Records Warranting Follow-Up" under "Results of Knock-Out Trademark Search" | Delete the header |
| "Internal planning discussion" as a footer | Delete |
| "This executive summary does not replace a current TSDR pull..." | Delete |
| "Calibrated expert view" under every rating | Delete, and keep one source note |

### 3.5 Illogical wording

- **Question:** Does it make sense as written, even when read in context?
- **Test:** Read the words carefully and check each relationship they claim.
- **Flag when:** words contradict each other, pair things that cannot pair, compress meaning until it is unreadable, or misstate a fact of the field.

| Illogical | Plain version |
|---|---|
| "Test, learn, and scale the most promising ideas" | "Develop, test, and scale the most promising ideas" |
| "Incubate adjacent offerings and business models that can expand the core." | "Develop related products and business models that expand the core business." |
| "New mobility concepts and changing behavior in cities." | "New transportation options and changing travel behavior in cities." |
| "79% Transit use back to pre-pandemic" | "Transit use has recovered to 79% of its pre-pandemic level." |
| "Revenue Growth Decline Expansion" | "Revenue Decline" |
| "PPP value comes with trade-offs to price, allocate and manage." | Name the actual trade-off: what the buyer gains and what it gives up |
| "Whole-life performance" | "Lifecycle performance" |
| "Innovation & service" or "Revenue & public trust" | One topic per label, or two closely related words |
| "Retain fare policy, data access, equity protections and transparency." | A complete sentence that says who keeps what |
| "HVO, renewable diesel, FAME blends" listed as drop-in fuels | "HVO and renewable diesel" (FAME is a different category) |
| "Broad, relevant, practice-based knowledge in the depth of Agile frameworks" | "Deep, practical knowledge of Agile frameworks and Lean principles" |

