## 4. Rules by element

| Element | Write it like this | Avoid |
|---|---|---|
| Title | The slide's point as a plain statement with the key fact or number. "Results of Knock-Out Trademark Search" | Taglines, commands, dashes, passive voice, vague verbs such as "shaped by", "broadening", "anticipate" |
| Subtitle | One plain sentence that adds a fact the title lacks | Filler such as "Tangible outcomes across five priority areas" |
| Kicker above the title | Only the project or topic name from the brief, such as "PROJECT RENA" | Kickers that restate the title (PEAK SEASON 2025), department names nobody asked for, generic tags (GROWTH STRATEGY, DAILY WELLBEING) |
| Section and column headers | Generic nouns that match the content: Purpose, Key takeaways, Focus area, Typical value chain | Commands, "Why X matters", "What drove Y", headers that restate the title |
| Row and card labels | Concrete names from the source: Exports, Delivery certainty, Advisory | One-word abstractions (Persistence), unrelated "&" pairs, synonym slashes, added modifiers |
| Short descriptors under a label | Plain words that describe the item | Ad-copy lists ("Iconic gas, hybrid & adventure"), overlapping words ("software & digital") |
| Body bullets | One specific fact or cause and effect, active voice, subject present, a number where one exists | Fragment openers, soft verbs such as "helps" or "supports" with no mechanism, lines that repeat the header |
| Action bullets | Verb, exact object, and the standard or location | Vague objects ("refresh cycles", "change protocol"), stacked nouns |
| Metric tiles | Number plus plain noun: "50+ Active customers" | Jargon units ("Consulting FTEs"), tiles with no number |
| Chart titles and axes | What the chart measures, in plain units | "Illustrative growth mix", "Conceptual activity rhythm", "Circadian time" |
| Callouts | A short grammatical phrase with fitting words: "Unusually large NWC change" | Squeezed labels with mismatched words |
| Closing lines | Only a new fact or a decision | "Together, ...", "Key idea: ...", "Implication: ...", "This work contributed to ..." |
| Quotes | Real quotes from the source | Invented quotes that restate the bullets |
| Footers | Page number, or the plain topic of the deck | Meta notes (Internal planning discussion), vertical bars |
| Source notes | "Source: X." plus any definition in its own short sentence | Semicolons, hedging disclaimers, notes about what the source lacks |

