## 8. The line check

Run these in order on every title, label, bullet, cell and note. One failure means a rewrite.

1. **Characters.** No em dash, en dash pause, semicolon, vertical bar, arrow, in-line dot, label slash or colon chain.
2. **Frames.** No "X, not Y", "it's not X, it's Y", "Do Y, not X" or "rather than" contrast.
3. **Actor.** Every verb has a named actor doing it. No hidden passive.
4. **Titles and headers.** They describe. They never command. No "Why X matters" or "What drove Y".
5. **Slogan test.** Nothing that would fit on a billboard. No two-fragment taglines and no buzzword triads.
6. **Delete test.** Remove each word or phrase in turn. If the meaning holds, leave it out.
7. **Which-one test.** Every noun answers "which", "of what" and "to what". Every claim carries a number, a name or a mechanism.
8. **Out-loud test.** Each phrase sounds like natural speech. Verbs and adjectives fit their nouns. No stacks of three or more nouns.
9. **Pair test.** "&" and "and" join related words only. No two words that mean the same thing.
10. **Repeat test.** The line does not repeat the title, its own header, another bullet or the footer.
11. **Source test.** Each label and claim traces to the brief or source. No invented kickers, meta footers, hedging disclaimers, made-up quotes or unsupported specifics. Technical terms sit in the right category.
12. **Logic test.** The line makes sense as written, with no contradictions and no condensed shorthand that drops words.

